---

<CaseStudyOverlay {...toOverlayProps(autodeskSso)}>
  <ProseBlock
    heading="Autodesk acquires BuildingConnected"
    layout="stacked"
    image={platformDiagram}
    imageAlt="Autodesk Construction Cloud as a platform of point solutions: design tools, quantification, collaboration, project management, and BuildingConnected"
    imageCaption="BuildingConnected among Autodesk Construction Cloud’s point solutions."
    fit="contain"
  >
    <p>
      Autodesk acquired BuildingConnected in 2019. BuildingConnected focused on
      bid solicitation, serving both general contractors and subcontractors. By
      targeting the sending and receiving of requests for proposals,
      BuildingConnected grew organically into a large network of 1M+
      construction companies, with a wealth of data on trades and pricing.
    </p>
    <p>
      The two businesses ran on different models. BuildingConnected was a hybrid
      freemium product and a two-sided market: inviting off-network
      subcontractors led to organic growth and more value, and subcontractors
      and general contractors each owned their own data. Autodesk Construction
      Cloud primarily targeted larger enterprises, where general contractors
      provisioned accounts for their subcontractors and consultants and owned
      the data. Still, BuildingConnected fit well into a platform of point
      solutions, and it stood to benefit from Autodesk’s advanced tools and
      capabilities.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="“One Autodesk”"
    image={accPlatform}
    imageAlt="Autodesk Construction Cloud connecting Autodesk Build, BIM Collaborate, and Docs around shared construction workflows"
    imageCaption="Autodesk Construction Cloud, connecting Build, BIM Collaborate, and Docs."
  >
    <p>
      The goal for Autodesk was to create a unified construction software
      platform. And not only that, they were trying to create a more cohesive
      internal culture, centered on the idea of “One Autodesk”. During the
      acquisition, there had been talk of keeping BuildingConnected as a
      separate brand and platform (similar to, say, YouTube vs Google), but that
      clashed with the internal messaging, and became a source of tension.
      Eventually, it was decided that BuildingConnected should become fully
      embedded in the emerging Autodesk Construction Cloud platform.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock heading="My role">
    <p>
      I was a Senior User Experience Designer at Autodesk from 2019 to 2022,
      working on BuildingConnected. I planned and ran the research for this
      project with a cross-functional tiger team of design, product management,
      and UX research, and led the workshops that aligned the team on direction,
      research synthesis, priorities, and planning.
    </p>
    <p>
      I also designed the critical sign-up and sign-in flows, and developed a
      phased plan for rolling out Autodesk ID that took both the technical
      challenges and users’ incentives and behavior into account.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Why Autodesk ID"
    image={autodeskSignIn}
    imageAlt="The Autodesk ID sign-in page in a browser"
    imageCaption="The Autodesk ID sign-in page, the shared login every product would use."
  >
    <p>
      Autodesk ID is the unified authentication platform for all Autodesk
      products. The goal of any platform is common user provisioning,
      centralized data, and integrated features and workflows that strengthen
      one another. The BuildingConnected experience stood to benefit from
      centralized documents, structured “Sheets” files, and integration with
      “RFIs” (ie formal tracking of questions and feedback). Autodesk ID was a
      prerequisite for all of that.
    </p>
    <p>
      Product leadership asked us to form an experience-driven “perspective” on
      the best way to integrate with Autodesk ID. They needed to understand the
      possibility space, and they were nervous about the complexity of
      integrating.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock heading="Three possible approaches">
    <p>
      The first step was to understand what was possible. I did a knowledge-sync
      with a software architect on how authentication might work. From that
      conversation, I identified three approaches to compare. I put these
      options together with examples from familiar platforms to help frame the
      project for my team.
    </p>
    <p>Here’s a breakdown of the approaches:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        “Linked Accounts”, people would manage two accounts that share data and
        permissions, adding or removing an Autodesk ID in their user settings.
      </li>
      <li>
        “Twin Credentials” would let them sign in with either account, and make
        sign-up easy if they were already logged in.
      </li>
      <li>
        “One Account”, Autodesk ID would replace BuildingConnected’s own
        authentication, and existing users would need to opt in, or “migrate”,
        to the new system.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </ProseBlock>

  <AssetRow
    assets={[
      {
        image: linkedAccountsExample,
        alt: "Facebook’s apps and websites settings, an example of linked accounts from another product",
        caption: "Linked accounts, as in Facebook’s app settings.",
      },
      {
        image: twinCredentialsExample,
        alt: "Medium’s sign-in dialog with several sign-in options, an example of twin credentials from another product",
        caption: "Twin credentials, as in Medium’s several ways to sign in.",
      },
      {
        image: oneAccountExample,
        alt: "Google’s sign-in page, an example of one account shared across many products",
        caption: "One account, as in Google’s sign-in across products.",
      },
    ]}
  />

  <ProseBlock
    heading="A concern over friction"
    layout="stacked"
    image={crossroads}
    imageAlt="A sketch of a road splitting three ways, labeled possibility 1, 2, and 3"
    imageCaption="The choice we had to stress-test: commit to one approach, or wait."
  >
    <p>
      Stakeholders wanted us to “stress-test” these options. One Account seemed
      like a massive change, but the alternatives seemed like they could
      introduce complexity to the experience. They wanted the team to identify
      which options introduced the least “friction” to the user experience.
    </p>
    <p>
      With this in mind, I planned three rounds of prototyping and testing to
      explore the impacts of different options. But we also needed the right
      test case. With a project as open-ended as this, we needed to not only
      define what solutions to test, but also what user flows were most
      important to test.
    </p>
    <p>
      There were various user-flows related to authentication, but in talking
      with the team, I urged them to focus on sign-up flows. I saw sign-up as
      critical for two reasons:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Sign-up flows are often a point of friction because they require various
        inputs and choices.
      </li>
      <li>
        As a freemium, network-driven service, sign-up was critical to
        BuildingConnected’s growth and long-term success.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>With the team in alignment, we set out to conduct user testing.</p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="What testing showed"
    layout="stacked"
    image={oneAccountPrototype}
    imageAlt="A One Account prototype: a construction drawing with a prompt to activate an Autodesk account to use a new takeoff tool"
    imageCaption="A One Account prototype, offering Autodesk ID in exchange for a new takeoff tool."
  >
    <p>
      For Linked Accounts, we tested a hypothetical messaging service where
      users were invited to view messages but had to use a secondary service to
      access attachments. It felt frustrating and redundant, and it would be
      difficult to introduce during sign-up.
    </p>
    <p>
      Twin Credentials used the same scenario with “twin” sign-up buttons. That
      removed the redundancy, but users saw no reason to use the secondary
      sign-up.
    </p>
    <p>
      For One Account, we tried getting users to migrate mid-flow by offering a
      real value proposition to real users. A clear value meant less resistance
      and friction. We also learned that there was little awareness of Autodesk,
      and that email verification was tricky.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Synthesis and insights"
    image={synthesisWorkshop}
    imageAlt="Sticky notes from a synthesis workshop, placed on a target diagram by how important they were"
    imageCaption="A synthesis workshop, sorting findings by how much they mattered."
  >
    <p>
      After testing, I led the team in workshops to analyze our data and
      synthesize insights. General awareness of Autodesk and its products, other
      than AutoCAD, was low. Some approaches were redundant and required ongoing
      management. One Account could be inserted almost seamlessly into sign-up.
      And users were unlikely to opt in to Autodesk ID, but very open to it when
      it was paired with a new feature.
    </p>
    <p>
      With these in mind, we agreed to recommend One Account as the general
      approach. It would require a potentially disruptive prompt asking existing
      users to adopt Autodesk, but ultimately we agreed that a one-time
      disruption was better than the ongoing cognitive load and friction of the
      other options.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="The invitation-to-bid flow"
    image={itbOriginal}
    imageAlt="The original invitation-to-bid page, asking the invitee to create an account to view the request for proposal"
    imageCaption="The original invitation-to-bid page, the on-ramp for new subcontractors."
  >
    <p>
      General contractors used BuildingConnected to send invitations to
      subcontractors asking them to review and bid on projects. Not every
      subcontractor they might invite was on the network yet, so invitees
      without an account were prompted to sign up. That was the growth engine
      behind growing BuildingConnected’s network and ultimately their value as a
      company. But now, it would be not just an on-ramp to BuildingConnected but
      potentially to the Autodesk Construction Cloud.
    </p>
    <p>
      The user-flow: invitees received an email that took them to a landing
      page, where they were prompted to sign up to see the full details of the
      project. If they entered an email that already had an account, they would
      just sign in. If the email was not recognized, they would need to sign up.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Mapping the scenarios"
    layout="stacked"
    image={scenarioMatrix}
    imageAlt="A two-by-two matrix of BuildingConnected and Autodesk account status, showing whether each user would need to sign in or sign up, and create or link an Autodesk account"
    imageCaption="Every mix of BuildingConnected and Autodesk account, and what each person would have to do."
    fit="contain"
  >
    <p>
      With Autodesk ID, we had to add more scenarios to this workflow.
      Authentication and sign-up would have to redirect through Autodesk for
      some users and BuildingConnected’s legacy systems for others. I created a
      matrix of scenarios, crossing whether someone had a BuildingConnected
      account with whether they had an Autodesk account. Users who already had
      both platforms would need to connect/merge their existing accounts, while
      other users had to create one account find/sign-in the existing account.
      Users who had neither would have to create everything at once.
    </p>
    <p>
      We started with the “happy path”—the case where users were new to both
      platforms. If we could define an experience for new users that felt like a
      single unified flow, we could protect the platform’s growth potential and
      all other flows would be edge-cases or temporary states until final
      migration.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="A minimal design change"
    image={itbAutodeskSignUp}
    imageAlt="The invitation-to-bid page with a branded Autodesk ID sign-up button, an “I’ve used BuildingConnected before” secondary button, and an escape-hatch sign-in link, annotated"
    imageCaption="The same page, with an Autodesk ID sign-up and a clear path for existing users."
  >
    <p>
      As the lead designer, I started work on the invitation-to-bid page. I
      focused on making subtle changes, to minimize disruption. I introduced a
      new, branded Autodesk button to signal the connection and the redirect to
      their domain.
    </p>
    <p>
      Next, I changed the language of “Already have an account?” to “I’ve used
      BuildingConnected before”, making it more direct and intuitive and
      avoiding confusion over which account we were referencing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Testing showed the need for an “escape hatch”, so I also added an explicit
      sign-in button to the header for users who might be thrown off by the new
      language.
    </p>
    <p>The flow for new users:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Enter your email and click “Sign-up with Autodesk”</li>
      <li>Be redirected to Autodesk to enter a password and name</li>
      <li>
        Redirect back to BuildingConnected to fill-in final details and view the
        RFP.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>Overall: clear, no unexpected detours or decisions. Seamless.</p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Catching existing users"
    layout="stacked"
    image={signInFlow}
    imageAlt="A flow diagram of how the invitation page routes different users to Autodesk ID sign-up and sign-in paths"
    imageCaption="How the invitation page routes each kind of user into Autodesk ID."
    fit="contain"
  >
    <p>
      With the happy path defined, I proceeded to map a flow diagram for how we
      would handle different user flows. Using a simple check on the input
      field, we could catch whether someone already had an Autodesk ID or a
      BuildingConnected ID and direct them as needed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Incidentally, while mapping these flows, I identified a minor security
      vulnerability. Because we weren’t requiring users to verify their Autodesk
      account before accessing BuildingConnected, it would have been
      theoretically possible to “steal” someone’s BuildingConnected account. I
      proposed a solution and worked with a senior engineer to work out how to
      fix the issue.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock heading="In-app prompts">
    <p>
      New users would be given Autodesk ID automatically, but legacy users
      needed to be prompted. Adopting Autodesk ID would be a small, low risk
      decision, but it was still a decision that needed to be presented
      carefully. The next step was to design interstitials and pop-ups that
      could catch users at the right time.
    </p>
    <p>
      The interstitial appeared to users signing in from any touch point, not
      just invitations to bid. Based on testing, we knew that users needed some
      sort of incentive or reason to convert—prompts to convert without a clear
      rationale were ignored. Discussing with the team, we identified some value
      props: enhanced security features available in the Autodesk platform and
      the aspirational appeal of the evolving unified platform (alluding to
      future integrations).
    </p>
    <p>
      Users who didn’t see or ignored the interstitial could also be prompted
      with a simple pop-up (a teammate designed the pop-up initially, and I
      carried it forward). We exposed the prompts progressively, choosing
      cohorts of friendly companies in good standing and showing the prompts to
      increasingly larger groups.
    </p>
    <p>
      With these pieces designed, we had all scenarios covered. But for a
      sensitive context like this, we also needed a plan.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <AssetRow
    assets={[
      {
        image: signInInterstitial,
        alt: "The sign-in interstitial: “Take 30 seconds to set up Autodesk ID,” with an Enable Autodesk ID button and an option to do it later",
        caption: "The sign-in interstitial: 30 seconds to set up Autodesk ID.",
      },
      {
        image: autodeskIdPopUp,
        alt: "A “Get Autodesk ID!” pop-up over the BuildingConnected bid board",
        caption: "A later pop-up, for people who skipped the interstitial.",
      },
    ]}
  />

  <ProseBlock
    heading="A phased release plan"
    layout="stacked"
    image={releasePhases}
    imageAlt="The four-phase release plan, Groundwork, Pilot, Promote, and Migrate, gated by technical, UX, and adoption requirements, starting in January 2020"
    imageCaption="The release plan: Groundwork, Pilot, Promote, and Migrate, gated by thresholds rather than dates."
    fit="contain"
  >
    <p>
      There were many moving parts in this project and many little touch points.
      I could see that we needed a long-term plan. I came up with a multi-step
      plan for release and migration, and presented it to my team for feedback
      and buy-in. The plan had no dates. There was a lot of uncertainty around
      which future integrations we wanted to build and how users would react to
      the introduction of Autodesk, so I proposed a flexible timeline based on
      thresholds, not arbitrary dates.
    </p>
    <p>
      Phase I, Groundwork, gave engineering time to build basic infrastructure
      and meet the technical requirements for integration. I used this phase to
      design the invitation and prompt flows while engineering implemented
      back-end infrastructure.
    </p>
    <p>
      Phase II, Pilot, would manually enable Autodesk ID for friendly customers
      we knew would be open to migrating. We would test integration prototypes
      with them and monitor for friction and UX blockers. The goal would be to
      launch our MVP Autodesk integrations, in hope that we could use those to
      incentivize broader adoption.
    </p>
    <p>
      Phase III, Promote, would actively encourage people to opt in, using our
      high-value integrations as the incentive, until adoption met an agreed
      upon threshold (e.g. 80% of users).
    </p>
    <p>
      Phase IV, Migrate, would set a deadline once the bulk of users had moved
      and there was enough value to justify disrupting the remaining users. That
      meant an email and communications plan, an in-app countdown, removing the
      ways to sign in with BuildingConnected, and flows to catch users who
      missed the deadline.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="The broader unification effort"
    layout="stacked"
    image={sessionsOutOfSync}
    imageAlt="A diagram of Autodesk ID and BuildingConnected sessions falling out of sync because the two are on different domains"
    imageCaption="Separate domains let an Autodesk ID session and a BuildingConnected session fall out of sync."
  >
    <p>
      Autodesk ID was a first step in a much broader process. During my time at
      Autodesk, I also explored projects across various products, whether to
      adopt a common domain, and integrating documents and Sheets. A project was
      a central container of data and work in construction, but it worked very
      differently in different products, and the two companies’ business models
      turned out to have a huge impact on how projects and their data were
      handled. I analyzed the idea of a project across Autodesk Construction
      Cloud products, and mapped how a project’s dynamics, use cases, and
      relationships change over the lifecycle of a real-world construction
      project. That analysis showed how the conflicting business models were
      blocking unification, and how better models could improve use down-market.
    </p>
    <p>
      I also identified problems caused by separate domains, such as sign-in
      sessions falling out of sync, and presented the problem and potential
      fixes to product leadership. I eventually became something of a
      unification advocate. On a unification “road show,” I met with
      stakeholders from all over the division, sometimes learning about other
      products and other times explaining the challenges of unification and
      advocating change. I also presented my findings to product group leaders
      and to the division’s full design and research org.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock heading="Impact">
    <p>
      Today, 80% of BuildingConnected users sign in with Autodesk ID, with no
      significant drop-off in activity and no customer complaints through the
      migration. At the time of launch, BuildingConnected had over 1 million
      companies in its network and $56 Billion in bids passed through the
      platform each month.
    </p>
    <p>
      This work was critical for integrating with the Autodesk platform, which
      ultimately enabled high-value features such as Autodesk document storage
      and viewing of special. For years, the BuildingConnected product manager I
      worked with shared my projects-research artifacts with teams grappling
      with unification questions.
    </p>
    <p>
      As of 2026, the BuildingConnected platform is finally being absorbed into
      Autodesk Forma, the final phase of a long, complex process that started
      with Autodesk ID.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <EndMark logo={logoBadge} label="Autodesk 2020" />
</CaseStudyOverlay>
